\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Ordinary linear involutions}
\date{2026-10-20}

\p{Let #{R} be a commutative unital ring and #{A} an associative
unital \vocabk{#{R}-algebra}{ca-0016}. An
\newvocab{ordinary #{R}-linear involution} on #{A} is an
\vocabk{#{R}-linear map}{ca-001F} #{a\mapsto a^\dagger},
with #{\sigma=\operatorname{id}_R}, satisfying
##{1^\dagger=1,\qquad
   (ab)^\dagger=b^\dagger a^\dagger,\qquad
   (a^\dagger)^\dagger=a.}
Thus it reverses products, preserves sums and satisfies
#{(ra)^\dagger=ra^\dagger}. The involution on the coefficient ring
is explicitly the identity.}

\p{These are ordinary product-reversal laws: no sign depending on
degree occurs. Over #{\mathbb C}, this convention is bilinear and
does not introduce complex conjugation. A Hermitian adjoint needs
different coefficient data. Neither a grading nor freeness selects
an involution; the [counting adjoint](fdip-001H) below is constructed
from a specified pairing.}

\p{Mathlib separates the involutive law \leanref{InvolutiveStar},
product reversal \leanref{StarMul}, additive compatibility
\leanref{StarRing} and scalar compatibility \leanref{StarModule}.
Its \leanref{starRingOfComm} permits the identity involution on
a commutative ring. These interfaces support the stated convention;
the represented example requires its own proof.
For the library attribution, see \citek{mathlib2020lean}.}
